<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · West Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Wala State</h1><p class="st-meta">1650 CE – 1896 CE (approx.) · capital Wa</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Kingdom of north-western Ghana centred on Wa, ruled by a Mole-Dagbani-linked dynasty alongside an influential Muslim community.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Wala traditions describe the arrival in the 17th century of horsemen linked to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-dagbon">Dagbon</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/mamprugu">Mamprugu</a> royal lines, who joined with Muslim <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wangara">Dyula</a> traders and the local earth-priests to form a state around the town of Wa in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-volta">Black Volta</a> region.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The Wa Na was chosen in rotation from several royal gates, while Muslim families held important offices and the earth-priests kept control of land rites. Wa was a stage on routes linking the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niger-bend">Niger bend</a> with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akan">Akan</a> goldfields and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/hausa-kingdoms">Hausaland</a>, and its Friday mosques reflect a long tradition of Islamic scholarship.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In the 1880s and 1890s Wa was ravaged by the slave-raiding armies of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babatu">Babatu</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samori-ture">Samori Ture</a>. <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-kingdom">Britain</a> took control in the late 1890s, and Wa became part of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/northern-territories-of-the-gold-coast">Northern Territories of the Gold Coast</a>. The Wa Na still reigns.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="kingdom, 1650 CE – 1896 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 72 V40 L36 54 L50 28 L64 54 L78 40 V72 Z"/><path d="M22 80 H78"/><circle cx="50" cy="24" r="3"/><circle cx="22" cy="36" r="2.5"/><circle cx="78" cy="36" r="2.5"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">kingdom</p><p class="st-emblem-region">West Africa</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:92.54%;width:4.90%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1650 CE – 1896 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Wa</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Waale</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/ghana">Ghana</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Wa, Kingdom of Wa, Wala</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1773&amp;focus=wala-state" aria-label="Wala State on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4611 1802 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Wala State lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M4931 2058L4747 2063M4931 2058L4972 2079"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4747 2063h0M4972 2079h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4931 2058h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4931 2058h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Wa. <a href="/atlas?year=1773&amp;focus=wala-state">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
